{"text":"Recent work supports separating persistent preferences from current intent. [LSIDN (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.14743) uses separate encoders and denoising strategies: session-level interest evolution for long-term preferences, contrastive augmentation for short-term noise. [D2MFRec (2025)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-31190-z) combines global sequence modeling, recent-item attention, soft clustering into multiple interests, and learned fusion gates. [DSRec (2026 preprint)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21548) explicitly uses historical aggregation for stable preferences and inter-click time intervals for local intent, with separate state-space encoders and residual cross-fusion.

The closest match to satisfied purchase intent is [Learning to Forget (SIGIR 2026 Industry Track)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.12714). Its satiation-aware mechanism suppresses historical clicks associated with completed purchases, learns replenishment rhythms, and applies a time-sensitive gate that inhibits satisfied interests before gradually reactivating them. A time-to-next-purchase auxiliary objective learns product lifecycles. Its click/purchase distinction requires signals beyond an undifferentiated interaction history; an interaction alone cannot establish that a need was fulfilled.

For this benchmark, a practical hypothesis is a recent-window intent encoder plus several persistent-interest vectors, fused through a learned gate conditioned on observed time gaps, recent/history agreement, and sequence length. Optional decay or recovery of latent interests could approximate intent lifecycles using histories alone, but should be treated as an inferred mechanism, not observed satisfaction. Compare against SASRec through validation HR@10, with ablations for separate branches, multiple interests, timestamp conditioning, and suppression to establish which components help."}
